about this time of year we always hear  the phrase new year new me but how can  you make that commitment towards your  health in our buddy check report Denise  Valdez talks to experts who hope that  women will put their Breast Care at the  top of their lips  [Music]  when it comes to annual mammography  screening about 66 percent of women over  40 in our state are getting it done  regularly so in this new year a push to  make that commitment and schedule your  appointment  were below the national average for  screening and we could certainly do  better Carrie Harrington is the  executive director of the Nevada cancer  Coalition a non-profit dedicated to  cancer prevention early detection  treatment and survivorship we know that  screening saves lives if you can catch  breast cancer in its earlier stages it's  much easier to treat we hear it all the  time early detection is key but some  women face barriers that make it tough  to get a mammogram every year how are  women in Nevada doing when it comes to  getting that early detection and getting  those mammograms we know we have about 2  600 women that are diagnosed with a new  case of breast cancer and we lose about  440 of our friends family neighbors to  this disease each and every year in  Nevada so the Coalition works with  health care providers hospitals and  Advocates to reach women moment  one partner is Nevada Health Centers  which runs the mammo van a mobile  mammography unit reaching Rural and  Frontier parts of the state but it also  visits the valley pretty regularly  there's also the Women's Health  connection helping the uninsured and  underinsured or those who simply fall  through the cracks it's a program funded  by the CDC and some dollars from the  state of Nevada they provide breast  cancer screening and Diagnostics as well  as cervical cancer screening with so  many resources available the Coalition  hopes that Nevada women won't have a  reason to postpone their annual  screening and as we do every month we  encourage you to call your buddy to  remind them to do their breast self-exam  Denise Valdez live local
 

